<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=norfolk-four-course-system">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Farming and food</p><h1 class="enc-title">Norfolk four-course system</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">early 16th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norfolk">Norfolk</a> four-course system is a method of crop rotation in which four crops follow one another over a four-year cycle: wheat, turnips, barley, and clover or ryegrass. Unlike the earlier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-field-system">three-field system</a>, it has no fallow year. It was developed in the early 16th century in Waasland, in present-day northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, and popularised in the 18th century by the British agriculturist Charles Townshend. Its fodder crop, turnips, and its grazing crop, clover, allowed livestock to be kept year-round, and it was a key development in the British Agricultural Revolution.</p></div>
